# port-patches.sh: glm/nucleus-race @ 3155e0ea134a over glm/zig-engine-fixes-on-186 @ e027c49cd06d (commits 3022ee57d656..069c49ffd2dc); regenerate with scripts/port-patches.sh, do not edit
glm_cuda: KV shard device paths: owner-computed decode and glm_shard.cu, a prompt chunk's history fetch, prompt borrowing and kept states' carries; TF_GLM_KV_SHARD=1 on (port of GLM 0118, parts 3-5)

The GPU side of KV sharding on the host side's parts: each stream's latent
rows are stored once, on the rank whose part holds them, so the pair's KV pool
holds more tokens in the same memory (1.83x measured, below).

- Decode (cuda_shard_decode, glm_shard.cu): per DSA layer each rank runs the
  existing _seg_chunks + _merge for its own heads of the rows it owns, then,
  after an exchange of the queries, the peer's heads of those rows, and the
  outputs come back to the rank that serves them; a row a rank does not own
  reads no key and its output is replaced whole. Same launches over the same
  key bytes in the same order: bit for bit the unsharded result.
  glm_shard.cu adds the byte movers (counts, select, mark, sums, offsets,
  scan, compact, renumber); it is optional, as glm_kda is, and a build
  without it refuses TF_GLM_KV_SHARD=1 at start.
- A prompt chunk's history fetch (cuda_shard_prompt): the owner sends the
  history rows the chunk's selection names (all of them under the dense
  limit) into the other rank's copy, which renumbers its tokens and runs the
  same captured kernels over it, in blocks of whole tiles when the union is
  large; both ranks cut the same blocks from the replicated selection.
- Prompt borrowing (TF_GLM_KV_SHARD_TWINS): a fresh prompt's fill borrows a
  twin extent in the other part for its whole pass, so both ranks fill
  locally and nothing is fetched; it goes back before the first decode
  round, and a part out of room fills through the history fetch instead.
- Kept states (copies outside the pool) hold their latent rows only on the
  rank that owned the stream (their home part); a resume moves its fresh
  extent home when the home part has room, else carries the rows to the new
  owner (TF_GLM_KV_SHARD_CARRY, 2,048 tokens or more), else prefills from 0.
- cuda_shard.gpu_paths on: TF_GLM_KV_SHARD=1 starts. Counters on /health
  kv_shard and /metrics: owner rounds and bytes, prompt fetches, rows,
  bytes, syncs and blocked fetches, twins made and revoked, carries and what
  they moved, refusals. The v1 disk tier stays off when sharded (a warning
  on rank 0); spill v2 with sharding is refused by spill v2's own admission
  until the next patch.

Switches: as the host side's (TF_GLM_KV_SHARD 0 by default; _CARRY, _TWINS on
with it; _CHECK 1024). Off, every byte is as before. Tests (host, no GPU): the
plans, the Triton classes and two-rank models of decode, the history fetch and
the carries against the unsharded result, mutants; tools/glm_shard_emulate
runs glm_shard.cu's kernels on the host; zig build glm-shard-check builds the
GPU check of the row blocks.

Measured on the pair (g2, round 2's image): with KV_SHARD on, exact 39/39
identical to the base engine (8 streams, a 99,899-token prompt); the pool
1,511,424 tokens in 2 parts against 825,344 unsharded, 1.83x in the same
memory; the shared-prompt fan-out filled once; counters: owner_rounds 2,914
(18.6 GB exchanged), twins_made 74, prompt_fetches 605 (3.1 GB, 0 blocked),
checks 120, transfers_failed 0, carries 0 (no pressure made). With TWINS=0
(every prompt through the history fetch): 39/39. Decode, sharded against
unsharded (bench_decode.py, 384 tokens, 3 reps, not interleaved; reps vary up
to +/-4%): 1 stream 51.2 vs 53.2 (-3.7%), 2: 66.7 vs 68.8 (-3.1%), 4: 89.0 vs
92.5 (-3.8%), 8: 119.3 vs 121.0 (-1.4%). glm_shard.cu compiles with the
Sparks' nvcc 13.0 for sm_121.

Credits: Robert Wojciechowski (GitHub @wojo, X @rwojo); GLM patch 0118,
MiaAI-Lab/GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3-2x-DGX-Sparks-TensorFold#152.
